<%#
  The condition a figure was measured under -- see docs/custom-attribute-qualifiers.md.

  A select rather than radio buttons, for two reasons. The list runs to seven values for a
  tolerance, which is long for a radio row. And the blank option can be chosen again after a
  value was set, which a radio group cannot do: "not stated" has to stay reachable, because a
  contributor who learns that a source states no condition must be able to say so.

  The blank option posts an empty string. The controller removes the key rather than storing it, so
  an entry never carries `"qualifier": ""` -- which is neither a condition nor absent, and which
  every reader of the qualifier would have to special-case.

  No conversion happens when this changes, unlike the unit radios beside it: a different
  condition is a different measurement, so the number stays as the contributor typed it.
  entity_form.js converts only controls whose name ends in [unit], so nothing here opts out.
%>
<% field_id = "product[custom_attributes][#{custom_attribute.label}]-qualifier" %>
<div class="EntityFormAttribute-qualifier">
  <label for="<%= field_id %>" class="Form-label">
    <%= t("custom_attribute_qualifier.label") %>
  </label>
  <select class="Form-input" name="<%= "product[custom_attributes][#{custom_attribute.label}][qualifier]" %>" id="<%= field_id %>">
    <option value=""><%= t("custom_attribute_qualifier.not_stated") %></option>
    <% selected = product.custom_attributes&.dig(custom_attribute.label, 'qualifier') %>
    <% custom_attribute.qualifiers.each do |qualifier| %>
      <option value="<%= qualifier %>"<%= " selected" if qualifier == selected %>>
        <%= t("custom_attribute_qualifiers.#{qualifier}") %>
      </option>
    <% end %>
  </select>
</div>
